/**
 * @file groups.dox
 *
 * The two layers this repository is built around, and which file belongs to
 * which. Doxygen has no way to infer the boundary from the include graph, so
 * it is declared here once rather than repeated in every header: adding a
 * header to a layer is one line in this file.
 *
 * The boundary itself is normative and is argued in ADR-0001,
 * `docs/adr/0001-kernel-adapter-boundary.md`, which this site publishes.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup kernel Kernel / native API
 *
 * The backtest and forward-execution engine, and the API a host drives it
 * with. Nothing here knows what PineScript is: a host declares a
 * `NativeRunSpec`, implements the `NativeStrategyHost` callbacks, submits
 * `native_order::Request` values and reads back events, trades and a report.
 * TradingView parity is not a property of this layer — it is a policy the
 * @ref pine_adapter applies on top of it.
 *
 * Start at @ref native_host.hpp for the callback surface, @ref
 * native_run_spec.hpp for what a run declares, @ref native_order.hpp for the
 * request and event vocabulary, and @ref native_c_api.h for the same surface
 * in C. `examples/native/` builds its runnable hosts against exactly these
 * headers and nothing else.
 */

/**
 * @defgroup pine_adapter Pine adapter (TradingView-parity policy layer)
 *
 * The layer that turns a transpiled PineScript strategy into kernel commands
 * and reproduces TradingView's own behaviour where it differs from a neutral
 * engine — order priority within a bar, intraday caps, entry admission,
 * rounding thresholds, the quirks a parity corpus measures.
 *
 * It is a *consumer* of @ref kernel, never a privileged one: every rule here
 * is expressible through the same public native API a third-party host uses.
 * A reader migrating away from PineScript wants @ref kernel; a reader chasing
 * a TradingView divergence wants this layer.
 */

